review_comments.body:
`esbuild` has a `--banner` option which is their version of `--preamble`: https://esbuild.github.io/api/#banner. But yeah if Terser can generate an ESM minified file that’s smaller, sure, go with that.

Webpack has some good examples of various `"exports"` configurations. This one looks like your case: https://webpack.js.org/guides/package-exports/#providing-commonjs-and-esm-version-stateful

So using your proposed structure and based on https://webpack.js.org/guides/package-exports/#example-1, maybe something like this (just for the main version for now, and slim will be a copy):

```json
{
  "type": "module",
  "exports": {
    "node": {
      "development": {
        "module": "./dist/module/jquery.module.js",
        "import": "./dist/module-wrapper.js",
        "require": "./dist/script/jquery.js"
      },
      "production": {
        "module": "./dist/module/jquery.module.min.js",
        "import": "./dist/module-wrapper.min.js",
        "require": "./dist/script/jquery.min.js"
      },
      "default": "./dist/commonjs-wrapper.cjs"
    },
    "development": "./dist/module/jquery.module.js",
    "production": "./dist/module/jquery.module.min.js",
    "script": "./dist/script/jquery.min.js",
    "default": "./dist/module/jquery.module.min.js"
  }
}
```

With wrapper files like this:

```js
// dist/module-wrapper.js
export * from "./script/jquery.js";

// dist/module-wrapper.min.js
export * from "./script/jquery.min.js";

// dist/commonjs-wrapper.cjs
module.exports = require("./script/jquery.js");
```

I put these one level up because they’re just wrapper files, and also only intended for Node; but you can move them wherever they make the most sense to you (and you can move `dist/commonjs-wrapper.cjs` into `script` if you want to avoid the `.cjs` extension, but I think it’s kind of appropriate here because this file really is _only_ for Node CommonJS).

These wrappers all assume you only want to provide a default export. You could also provide both a default and named exports. For example, for an ESM wrapper:

```js
import package from "./script/jquery.js";
export default package;
export const { jQuery, $ } = package;
```

Or from the CommonJS wrapper:

```js
const package = require("./script/jquery.js");
module.exports = package;
module.exports.jQuery = package.jQuery;
module.exports.$ = package.$;
```

The CommonJS wrapper has an explicit `module.exports.jQuery` so that it triggers Node’s CommonJS named exports detection, so that Node ESM users can write `import { jQuery } from "jquery"` and it will work, even though the export is a CommonJS file.

It’s uncommon to use minified files with Node so I left the unminified version as the default for Node users; but the minified one for others, so that bundlers pick the minified build by default.

> wouldn’t that mean we have to attach them to the jQuery object, making us have `jQuery.jQuery === jQuery` & `jQuery.$ === jQuery`?

So I did some testing. All of these assertions return true.

```shell
mkdir test && cd test
npm install jquery
touch wrapper.cjs consumer.cjs wrapper.mjs consumer.mjs
```

```js
// wrapper.cjs
const pkg = require("jquery");
module.exports = pkg;
module.exports.jQuery = pkg;
module.exports.$ = pkg;

// consumer.cjs
const wrapped = require("jquery");
const wrapper = require("./wrapper.cjs");
console.assert(wrapper === wrapped, 1);
console.assert(wrapper.jQuery === wrapped, 2);
console.assert(wrapper.jQuery.jQuery === wrapped, 3);

// wrapper.mjs
import pkg from "jquery";
export default pkg;
export const jQuery = pkg;
export const $ = pkg;

// consumer.mjs
import wrapped from "jquery";
import wrapper, { jQuery } from "./wrapper.mjs";
console.assert(wrapper === wrapped, 1);
console.assert(jQuery === wrapped, 2);
console.assert(jQuery.jQuery === undefined, 3);
```

So you’re right _for the CommonJS wrapper,_ because that’s how CommonJS works, but not for the ESM wrapper. The `module.exports.jQuery = pkg;` line not only creates the `jQuery` module export but also adds a `jQuery` property to the `jQuery` object, because that’s what `module.exports` has already been assigned to be. I guess the question is whether this is a problem; they’re all references to the same object, so we don’t have a hazard.

Looking again at the Webpack example, their CommonJS example was only to serve production versus development versions based on `process.env.NODE_ENV` as a fallback for when the user isn’t using `--conditions`. I don’t think we need to do that; we can just serve the unminified version as our default for Node, and the minified one can be enabled only via `--conditions=production`. Therefore we wouldn’t need a CommonJS wrapper at all:

```json
{
  "type": "module",
  "exports": {
    "node": {
      "production": {
        "module": "./dist/module/jquery.module.min.js",
        "import": "./dist/module-wrapper.min.js",
        "require": "./dist/script/jquery.min.js"
      },
      "development": {
        "module": "./dist/module/jquery.module.js",
        "import": "./dist/module-wrapper.js",
        "require": "./dist/script/jquery.js"
      },
      "module": "./dist/module/jquery.module.js",
      "import": "./dist/module-wrapper.js",
      "require": "./dist/script/jquery.js"
    },
    "production": "./dist/module/jquery.module.min.js",
    "development": "./dist/module/jquery.module.js",
    "script": "./dist/script/jquery.min.js",
    "default": "./dist/module/jquery.module.min.js"
  }
}
```

```js
// module-wrapper.js (and module-wrapper.min.js is identical other than importing jquery.min.js)
import pkg from "./script/jquery.js";
export default pkg;
export const { jQuery, $ } = pkg;
```

With this approach, we can get both the named exports for ESM consumers without adding new properties on the `jQuery` object. Caveat though that I haven’t tested the `"exports"` stuff so treat this as a rough draft to amend with testing.

